<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Estácio de Sá</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1520">1520</a>–1567</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Estácio de Sá was a Portuguese soldier, nephew of the governor-general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mem-de-sa">Mem de Sá</a>, who founded the city of São Sebastião do <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> on 1 March 1565 at the foot of Sugarloaf Mountain. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temimino">Temiminó</a> allies he fought to drive the French and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupinamba">Tupinambá</a> allies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanabara-bay">Guanabara Bay</a>, ending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> Antarctique. He died of an arrow wound received in the decisive battle of 1567.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/estacio-de-sa.jpg" alt="Estácio de Sá" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Junius · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Funda%C3%A7%C3%A3o_do_Rio_de_Janeiro.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Estácio de Sá was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santarem-portugal">Santarém, Portugal</a>, in 1520. The Portuguese crown sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> to make war on the French colonists commanded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-durand-de-villegaignon">Nicolas Durand de Villegaignon</a>, who had settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanabara-bay">Guanabara Bay</a> in 1555 in the colony known as France Antarctique. He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a>, with two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galleon">galleons</a> in 1564, where his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mem-de-sa">Mem de Sá</a> was Governor General of the colony.</p><p>After long preparations, and with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-da-nobrega">Manuel da Nóbrega</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-anchieta">José de Anchieta</a>, he sailed in 1565 with an attack force from São Vicente, the first Portuguese settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. The settlement he founded near Sugarloaf Mountain became the base of his military operations against the French and their Indigenous allies. Reinforcements later came by sea from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>, sent by his uncle, and with them Sá led a decisive and successful attack on the fortification of Uruçú-mirim on 20 January 1567. An arrow pierced his eye in the fighting, and he died of the wound on 20 February 1567.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sá was buried in the church of Saint Sebastian in the encampment he had founded. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> grew, his remains were moved to a new church of Saint Sebastian on the Castelo. Scholars working for Emperor Pedro II rediscovered them in 1839, and in 1862, when the church was being rebuilt, some of his bones were exhumed in the emperor's presence and placed in an urn.</p><p>As the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, now the second largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, he has given his name to many places and institutions there, among them the Estácio neighbourhood of the city, the samba school Grêmio Recreativo Escola de Samba Estácio de Sá and the private Universidade Estácio de Sá, which owns a football club of the same name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Estácio de Sá” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mem de Sá (1557–1573) defeated the Indigenous peoples around Salvador and, with the Jesuits, expelled the French from France Antarctique; his nephew <b>Estácio de Sá</b> founded Rio de Janeiro in 1565.</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The city of São Sebastião do Rio de Janeiro, founded by <b>Estácio de Sá</b> in 1565, was moved to the Morro do Castelo.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-rio-de-janeiro">Captaincy of Rio de Janeiro</a></li><li><blockquote>His nephew <b>Estácio de Sá</b> founded Rio de Janeiro in 1565 and, with Portuguese and Temiminó forces, drove the remaining French and their Tupinambá allies from the bay by 1567.</blockquote><a href="/state/france-antarctique">France Antarctique</a></li><li><blockquote>Mem de Sá expelled the French from France Antarctique, and his nephew <b>Estácio de Sá</b> founded Rio de Janeiro in 1565.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-brazil">State of Brazil</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santarem-portugal">Santarém</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.24,-8.69&amp;year=1550" aria-label="Santarém on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Estácio de Sá"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4427 4192h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4427 4192h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.24,-8.69&amp;year=1550">What was here in 1550? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
